    "insert": "The provided exposé discusses the integration of AI into healthcare with focus on the creation and evaluation of Large Language Models that can be used for diagnosis and creation of individual treatment plans. While current research only focusses on the general models, which are included in ChatGPT, this paper aims at evaluating multiple LLMs and finetuning them to achieve even higher accuracy and efficiency. It is also planned to develop a new model that only focuses on healthcare diagnosis as it is expected to outperform even the modified models. To achieve this, it is planned to collaborate with medical experts for the creation of datasets and evaluation of the different models performances and reasoning.\n\nI like how your exposé includes all the necessary aspects from the assignment and follows a clear line of thought. This is supported by the subheadings which help to structure your text. Nevertheless, the clarity could be improved even further by including more headings or linebreaks (e.g. \"… newly developed LLMs. [new paragraph] Potential difficulties…\"). The language of your exposé is appropriate for most of the text. Yet, some sentences seem to be too colloquial to match the purpose of the exposé and style of the rest of the text. By aligning the choice of words of the passage I marked in the editor the perceived confidence in the entire text would be strengthened. It is a good idea to explain the basic concepts of your approach. Yet, explaining what a NLP corpus is and how LLMs are trained in general is not necessary for the target audience and does not contribute to answering your research questions. I therefore suggest removing this part. By explaining the necessity of your work and afterwards how you plan to deal with potential problems is still stringent.\n\nOne important goal of the exposé is to forumlate detailed research question that can be evaluated objectively. Therefore the research question and methodology should be phrased precisely and include metrics to measure success. I therefore suggest rephrasing RQ1 to include concrete metrics (e.g. F1-Score to combine and evaluate both recall and precision of your results). Although it is an area of active research you should also focus on specific aspect of LLM reasoning that are relevant to the problem (e.g. Chain-Of-Thoughts of a LLM). This would allow you to formalize your evaluation and further strengthen your claims. The same applies to RQ2. The term efficient is ambiguous. I recommend defining \"efficient\" in this context (e.g. how much the performance can be improved with limited data). Additionally, while you mention using the accuracy to evaluate the results, \"to answer questions from the medical field\" is to generic. By replacing this with the precise goal you formulated above (using LLMs for diagnosis and creation of individual treatment plans) the standalone expressiveness of the research question and ultimately of the entire research would be increased.\n\nAnother important goal of the exposé is to develop a research plan for the scope of a Bachelor-Thesis. While the evaluation and finetuning of existing models poses a great goal for this purpose the development of a new models exceeds the desired scope of work. This could lead to lack of time and ultimately harm your entire research. Nevertheless is important to investigate the quality of the existing models (especially the reasoning) and be a good starting points for developing a new model. By focussing solely on the fine-tuning and evaluation of existing models you would ensure the quality your research while staying in the scope of a Bachelor-Thesis.\n\nBy only focussing on the fine-tuning and evaluation of the existing models, you could focus more on answering the actual research questions. Currently, the state of the art, framework and methodology has to include both the existing models and the development of a new one. Therefore your text is only superficial in some places. Although as you mention understanding a models is the foundation of developing a new one, they have different perquisites and goals. By leaving out the development, the entire exposé could describe your approach to answer the research question in more detail. It also would free time in your schedule to further elaborate on your precise approach and in the end lead to more well-founded results.\n\nOverall, your exposé is well written and your sources are well chosen and directly contribute to answering your research questions.\n"
